pub fn entries() -> Vec<KernelRule> {
    vec![
        // ============================================================
        // -- fwht-butterfly-staging-ladder-warp-smem-register -- Batch
        // 166 / Plan 157 / Research 194 (fork #166 + rider; the in-tree
        // promotion trigger riir-ai Plan 600 / Issue 980).
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "fwht-butterfly-staging-ladder-warp-smem-register".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "Stage a wide in-block butterfly transform (Walsh-Hadamard, or any log-depth symmetric-sign butterfly) whose width EXCEEDS the all-register regime by its THREE-WAY span ladder, never as all-stages-through-shared-memory: classify each of the log2(W) stages by its partner span (the butterfly distance at that stage) and give each band the cheapest substrate that carries its traffic -- (1) spans below the warp width ride WARP SHUFFLES (partners are intra-warp: zero __syncthreads, zero smem round-trip); (2) the genuinely cross-warp spans stage through SHARED MEMORY with one barrier per stage -- at service geometries this is at most ~3 stages; (3) the remaining top stages are REGISTER-RESIDENT: factor the transform by data ownership so each lane completes the stages whose partners fall inside its own contiguous run as pure register butterflies -- the most data-heavy stages run with ZERO synchronization (the transform factors across the [runs x lanes] view; the per-run and cross-run stage groups commute as index relabelings, so the lane can finish its run's stages after the smem prefix without re-touching smem). The incumbent defect this replaces: a one-Hadamard-block-per-CUDA-block kernel (256 threads, W = 1024, __shared__ float smem[1024]) that runs ALL 10 stages through smem with TWO barriers per stage -- ~20 __syncthreads where the ladder needs ~6 -- syncing for stages whose traffic is intra-warp or entirely intra-lane. Measured (the fork, H100, folded bands -- EXPECTATION PRICING, not our silicon): block-per-row for W >= 512 gained +17% / +11% decode (9B / 2B); Hadamard was the LARGEST single decode kernel in their profile (25.8% of GPU time at block 4096); the Metal threadgroup twin (their #161, folded widths 512-2048) measured +4-36% decode on M5 Pro at a +2.2-4.8% pp COST -- a numerics risk price that does NOT transfer; our parity pins decide (below). Sign handling is NOT part of the ladder: fold the sign vector into the neighboring load/store (sign-first smem load; signs-on-store for the inverse) exactly as the incumbent already does -- the ladder changes stage PLACEMENT, never the sign arithmetic. BIT-IDENTITY IS THE GATE, not an obstacle: regrouping arithmetic per stage reorders f32 additions, and the FFMA-contraction precedent (riir-ai d07542ee3 -- the warp-butterfly ULP divergence was ptxas contraction, asm-guarded) says the compiler will see different contraction opportunities; any ladder rewrite must hold the in-repo parity pins verbatim (the riir-gpu G1 rotation-parity test + the fused family's bit-exact tests) or it is a numerics change requiring the lossy-surface walk (per-family conditional retention), never 'looks fine'. The fused rotate+quantize family carries a bit-identity contract -- 'butterfly: fwht_rotate_forward_f32 verbatim' -- so the rewrite goes through ONE shared device-inline butterfly body that all five fused kernels call, or rewrites all five in lockstep. In-repo consumers (the benchable axis, ours since riir-ai Plan 600 / Issue 980 landed 2026-09-19): riir-gpu deltanet_rotation_cudarc.rs / deltanet_rotation_cubecl.rs -- fwht_rotate_forward_f32 / fwht_rotate_inverse_f32 are exactly the incumbent shape (one block per transform, 256 threads, all stages through smem), the batched [p x width] prefill variants ride the same bodies, and bonsai2_hadamard is DEFAULT-ON (production posture, not an experiment); the M3 Metal twin is locally benchable. Rider: the fork's >2048-width extension (6f1c6908d) lands here only with a wider-block model -- every in-repo kernel is W = 1024 today (block_size: 1024 in the parity test). Distinct from fwht-shuffle-xor-butterfly-register-resident (Batch 96 -- the WHOLE transform register-resident, zero smem zero barriers, regime N <= LANES x register budget; that rule explicitly punts 'larger transforms fall to smem tiles or multi-pass schemes' WITHOUT content -- THIS rule is that fallback's decision content; compose: all-register below the budget, the ladder above it), from warp-shuffle-intrinsics-for-sub-warp-reduction and block-reduction-separate-scratch-runtime-strides (REDUCTION-scoped butterflies -- many-to-one convergence; a transform is a full exchange network where every lane emerges holding different outputs), and from smem-staging-gated-by-cross-thread-reuse (the general staging gate -- this is the transform-side instance with the register-resident top band). Source: PrismML-Eng/llama.cpp @ 1bc46a8a7 (#166, block-per-row W >= 512) + 6f1c6908d (rider); in-tree trigger riir-ai Plan 600 / Issue 980 / Research 194.  RIDER (Batch 250 slice 1 / Plan 254, count-neutral — the GRAPH-level fusion face + the fallback-fix face, prism fork #366 fixing #349, ad2328151, ancestor-verified at the scored pin 0082ac24a): above the ladder sits a DISPATCH half the fork landed in the graph builder — the signed transform is detected as a three-node subgraph and fused into ONE dispatch instead of running the elementwise sign pass separately: 'if (!ggml_can_fuse_subgraph(cgraph, node_idx, { GGML_OP_MUL, GGML_OP_RESHAPE, GGML_OP_MUL_MAT }, { node_idx + 2 }))' gates the fusion (mul -> reshape -> mul_mat with the hint param consumed by the mul_mat), the Hadamard identity asserted on the consumer side ('if (ggml_get_op_params_i32(mm, 1) != GGML_HINT_SRC0_IS_HADAMARD)') so a non-hint mul_mat refuses the fusion, and the sign vector is applied at ROW-LOAD inside the fused kernel — the elementwise pass skipped and its pipeline variant registered. The FALLBACK-FIX face: the fix corrects the signed fallback path the original fusion introduced (#349) — when the fused variant cannot serve a shape, the fallback must carry the SIGN application itself (a fallback that drops the sign pass inverts the transform's signs silently). The composition with THIS rule: the ladder places the stages, the graph fusion removes the SEPARATE sign dispatch entirely — the sign-first load/store fold (above) remains the in-kernel half, the subgraph detection is its graph-level sibling. Source: PrismML-Eng/llama.cpp (the scored fork) @ ad2328151 (#366); quotes verbatim at the pin this claim (the prismml-llama.cpp/prismml-b2 clones; NOT prismml-tip). Batch 166 / Plan 157.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: one transform per block, 256 threads, W = 1024 --\n// ALL log2(W) = 10 stages staged through shared memory, a barrier\n// pair per stage (~20 __syncthreads) although spans < 32 are\n// intra-warp and the top stages are intra-lane once lanes own\n// contiguous runs\nconst W: usize = 1024;\nconst THREADS: usize = 256;\n\nfn fwht_all_smem(smem: &mut [f32; W], tid: usize, signs: &[f32; W]) {\n    smem[tid] = input(tid) * signs[tid]; // sign-first load (keep this)\n    barrier();\n    let mut span = 1usize;\n    while span < W {\n        // every stage -- even span < 32 (intra-warp: a shuffle needs\n        // no smem round-trip) and even the top stages (intra-lane on\n        // the run view: no cross-thread traffic at all)\n        let a = smem[tid];\n        let b = smem[tid ^ span];\n        smem[tid] = if tid & span == 0 { a + b } else { b - a };\n        barrier();\n        barrier(); // 2 syncs x 10 stages, all through smem\n        span *= 2;\n    }\n    store(tid, smem[tid]);\n}\n\nfn input(tid: usize) -> f32 { 0.0 }\nfn store(tid: usize, x: f32) { let _ = (tid, x); }\nfn barrier() {}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: the staging ladder -- give each span band the\n// cheapest substrate that carries its traffic\nconst W: usize = 1024;\nconst THREADS: usize = 256;\nconst LANES: usize = 32;\nconst RUN: usize = W / THREADS; // contiguous elements per lane\n\nfn fwht_ladder(smem: &mut [f32; W], tid: usize, signs: &[f32; W]) {\n    // (0) prologue: sign-first load into smem (unchanged), ONE barrier\n    smem[tid] = input(tid) * signs[tid];\n    barrier();\n    // (1) sub-warp spans: warp shuffles -- zero barriers, zero smem\n    let lane = tid % LANES;\n    let mut span = 1usize;\n    while span < LANES {\n        let a = smem[tid];\n        let partner = shuffle_xor(a, span); // partner = lane ^ span\n        smem[tid] = if lane & span == 0 { a + partner } else { partner - a };\n        span *= 2;\n    }\n    // (2) the genuinely cross-warp spans: smem, ONE barrier each\n    //     (~3 stages at service geometries)\n    while span < W / (RUN * 2).max(LANES) {\n        let a = smem[tid];\n        let b = smem[tid ^ span];\n        smem[tid] = if tid & span == 0 { a + b } else { b - a };\n        barrier();\n        span *= 2;\n    }\n    // (3) the top stages: REGISTER-RESIDENT -- each lane owns RUN\n    //     contiguous elements and finishes its run's stages as pure\n    //     register butterflies: zero synchronization on the most\n    //     data-heavy stages\n    let base = tid * RUN;\n    let mut reg = [0.0f32; RUN];\n    for (i, r) in reg.iter_mut().enumerate() {\n        *r = smem[base + i];\n    }\n    let mut inner = 1usize;\n    while inner < RUN {\n        let mut j = 0;\n        while j < RUN {\n            let a = reg[j];\n            let b = reg[j + inner];\n            reg[j] = a + b;\n            reg[j + inner] = a - b;\n            j += inner * 2;\n        }\n        inner *= 2;\n    }\n    for (i, r) in reg.iter().enumerate() {\n        smem[base + i] = *r;\n    }\n    store(tid, smem[tid]);\n}\n\nfn input(tid: usize) -> f32 { 0.0 }\nfn store(tid: usize, x: f32) { let _ = (tid, x); }\nfn barrier() {}\nfn shuffle_xor(v: f32, mask: usize) -> f32 { let _ = mask; v }\n".to_string(),
        },
    ]
}
